  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Kostya wakes up in a good mood and misinterprets the situation completely, and we will listen to their dialogue in Russian first; do not worry about catching every single word.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Доброе утро, моя дорогая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это не доброе утро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Почему ты так на меня смотришь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я ждала, когда ты проснешься.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты проснулась и хочешь поиграть со мной?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, Костя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "А...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я сделал что-то не так?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты забыл, что мы обсуждали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я всё помню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда почему ты шутишь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я просто пытаюсь быть милым.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Доброе утро, моя дорогая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning, my dear.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'good' straight to 'expensive'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это не доброе утро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is not a good morning.' Nastya combines 'this' with 'morning' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Почему ты так на меня смотришь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why are you looking at me like that?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'why' with 'are staring'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я ждала, когда ты проснешься.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have been waiting for you to wake up.' The question word for 'when' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'wake up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты проснулась и хочешь поиграть со мной?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you awake and wanting to play with me?' Kostya combines 'you' with 'me' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Nastya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, Костя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, Kostya.' Nastya links 'no' directly with 'Kostya' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "А...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Ah...' Kostya answers concisely with the word for 'and'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я сделал что-то не так?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did I do something wrong?' Kostya links 'I' directly with 'did' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты забыл, что мы обсуждали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You forgot what we discussed.' Nastya links 'you' directly with 'forgot' to express the thought clearly. Then Kostya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я всё помню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I remember everything.' Kostya links 'I' directly with 'everything' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда почему ты шутишь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then why are you joking?' Nastya uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'are joking', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я просто пытаюсь быть милым.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am just trying to be nice.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'am trying' turns the action back onto the subject. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
